**Lemma 6.2 (finite native invariants and entire source reconstruction).** The
entire fiber of Definition 6.1 is exactly

$$
D=\{U(a,x,z):a\in\{\alpha,\beta\},\ x\notin\{T_n:n\in\mathbb N\},\ z\ne a\},
\qquad T_n=\rho^n\alpha.
\tag{FFM.24}
$$

Every such source satisfies the full old row and all records, and every original
satisfying that input is reconstructed as itself by (FFM.24).

Proof. The old exterior reaches $a=u|_L$ and $x=u|_{RL}$.
The absence at $LL$ forces $a$ to be a leaf. The four branch literals reveal

$$
u=\langle a,\langle x,\langle y,
       \langle\langle b,c\rangle,\langle z,z'\rangle\rangle\rangle\rangle\rangle.
\tag{FFM.25}
$$

Every endpoint in (FFM.22) is reached on this displayed skeleton at epoch zero,
and the original branches at $R,RL$'s ancestors survive the positive iterates.
The first four whole equalities force respectively $y=\rho^2x$, $b=\rho x$,
$c=x$, and $z=z'$. Thus the source is precisely (FFM.20). The first negative
says $F(x)\ne G(x)$, and the second says $z\ne a$.
The first conjunct of [SourceTransportCentralizer,
`source_transport_centralizer`][CENTRALIZER] gives on finite native trees

$$
\rho^2x=\langle\rho x,x\rangle\iff x=T_n\text{ for some }n\ge0.
\tag{FFM.26}
$$

This supplier, including its local `hinj` and `classify` proof, is finite-tree
classification. It is not a classification of completion solutions. Applying it
gives exactly the restriction on $x$ in (FFM.24), proving the first source direction.

Conversely choose any $a,x,z$ in (FFM.24). Its displayed constructors give all
literals and reached endpoints. The first four records are the defining native
identities and repeated $z$; (FFM.26) gives the first negative and $z\ne a$ the
second. The old class values are $a,x,s$, where
$s=\langle F(x),\langle G(x),\langle z,z\rangle\rangle\rangle$.
The non-atomic $x$ is a branch, hence differs from the leaf $a$; so does $s$.
For finite native leaf count $\lambda$, structural induction in (FFM.1) gives
$\lambda(\rho x)\ge\lambda(x)$. Consequently
$\lambda(s)>\lambda(F(x))\ge\lambda(x)$, so $s\ne x$. The three old classes
are therefore distinct, with exactly the required exterior and reached set.
This proves the converse including every old constraint. $\square$

